{
  // ⚠️ DELIBERATELY EXTENDS ASTRO'S PRESET, NOT ../../tsconfig.base.json.
  //
  // This is the one package in the repo that does not extend the repo base, so
  // here is the reasoning (do not "fix" it back without reading this):
  //
  // Astro type-checks `.astro` files through its own language tooling, which
  // imposes compiler options the repo base neither sets nor wants globally:
  // `jsx: "preserve"` (an .astro template is internally JSX), `allowJs`,
  // `isolatedModules`, `allowImportingTsExtensions`, and the `${configDir}`
  // include of `.astro/types.d.ts` — the GENERATED file (produced by
  // `astro sync`) that declares the `Astro` global, `Astro.locals`, and the
  // content-collection types. Extending the base and re-adding all of that by
  // hand would be a strictly worse copy of `astro/tsconfigs/strict`, and would
  // drift.
  //
  // ⚠️ The `API` Service Binding is NOT typed there, and there is no
  // `Astro.locals.runtime.env` to find — that API was REMOVED in Astro v6.
  // Bindings come from `worker-configuration.d.ts` (generated by
  // `wrangler types`, committed alongside, matching apps/api's convention) and
  // are reached via `import { env } from "cloudflare:workers"`; see
  // src/lib/api.ts.
  //
  // Nothing is given up by doing this. The base's substantive guarantees are
  // `strict`, `verbatimModuleSyntax`, and `moduleResolution: "bundler"` — and
  // `astro/tsconfigs/strict` sets all three identically (it is `base.json` +
  // `strict: true`). The only real differences are `target: ESNext` vs the
  // base's ES2022, which is irrelevant under `noEmit`, and the base's
  // `types: []`, which would be actively wrong here: it exists to keep stray
  // `@types/*` out of the Workers runtime, whereas this package NEEDS Astro's
  // ambient client types to resolve the `Astro` global.
  //
  // The base's former `"files": []` is gone on purpose too — it was a
  // placeholder so an empty `apps/web` could typecheck (TS18003) before this
  // task added any source. There are real sources now, and leaving it would
  // silently check NOTHING.
  //
  // Relatedly, this package's `typecheck` script is `astro check`, NOT the
  // `tsc --noEmit` every other package runs: `tsc` does not recognize the
  // `.astro` extension, so it would quietly check `src/lib/api.ts` alone and
  // pass while every page went unexamined. `astro check` type-checks the
  // templates and their frontmatter, and implies `astro sync`.
  "extends": "astro/tsconfigs/strict",
  "include": [".astro/types.d.ts", "**/*"],
  "exclude": ["dist"]
}
